React re-renders a child component whenever its parent re-renders, even if the props are identical. That's the default behavior.

On top of that, if you're creating new objects or functions as props each time the parent renders, React sees those as *different* props (different references), so it would re-render the child anyway.

**Common culprits:**

**The parent is re-rendering.** If the parent component's state or props change, it renders, and all children render with it. The fix is to wrap the child with `React.memo` — it skips the re-render if props haven't changed.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ name }) => {
  return <div>{name}</div>;
});
```

**Props are fresh objects each time.** If you pass an object or array created on the fly, it's a new reference every time, so even `React.memo` won't help. Use `useMemo` to keep the same reference:

```jsx
// Bad — new object every render
<Child data={{ id: 1, name: "Alice" }} />

// Good — same object reference if id/name don't change
const data = useMemo(() => ({ id: 1, name: "Alice" }), []);
<Child data={data} />
```

**Callback props.** Same issue with functions — use `useCallback` to keep the reference:

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => { /* ... */ }, []);
<Child onClick={handleClick} />
```

